hello I am Raphael Bennett I am a  postdoc at the analytical research and  development department at Merck in the  Rahway site and today I would like to  talk to you about my current exploits  with supercritical fluid chromatography  specifically with the newer  pharmaceutical modalities and let's get  started so some of the newer modalities  in the pharmaceutical industry have  included carbohydrates nucleotides  peptides proteins polymers and bio  conjugates  now these analytes are very diverse in  structure many of them hydrophilic and  usually one form of chromatography  doesn't cover everything so it's  important to have experts in all  different forms of chromatography  despite the fact that usually you have  reverse phase chromatography as your  go-to but or more polar analytes um our  PLC tends to not be the best way to go  sometimes to pick up the slack on this  you have things like hydrophilic  interaction chromatography however if  you're doing purification as you would  tend to do in the pharmaceutical  industry  heylook methods are oftentimes difficult  to scale furthermore if you have more  charged analytes that you want to deal  with you have ion exchange methods which  are very nice as far as using oftentimes  an aqueous type mobile phases but  because you have non-volatile salts  involved these methods are oftentimes  not mass spectrometry friendly and for  purification purposes you may have an  additional and desalting step so one of  the methods for purification oftentimes  used is supercritical fluid  chromatography to address these concerns  so the supercritical fluid  chromatography generally involves the  use of compressed co2 as part of the  mobile phase what this enables is high  mass transfer kinetics and faster flow  rates which means that you'll have  faster analytical methods and this  translates to faster purification which  can help you with in terms of cost  savings in terms of less liquid solvent  usage because a lot of your liquid  solvent is steady tap as co2 therefore  faster purification and reduced waste so  usually a greener method however  supercritical fluid chromatography when  it first began using pure co2 it's the  mobile phase was restricted to the two  nonpolar analytes as time went on and  technology grew you had the introduction  of modifiers usually alcoholic in nature  that would be very miscible with the co2  mobile phase these modifiers as you go  along to more polar analytes would  include additives sometimes water these  additives could be acidic or basic but  some polar entity that enables a better  separation however peptides proteins and  nucleic acids nucleotides still to this  day present a problem for SFC that many  researchers are still trying to find  answers to so with that in mind I will  talk about the polar modalities I have  encountered and answers in which I have  found or addressing each of these  including carbohydrates nucleotides  peptides and proteins and for that last  section I will go and delve a little  deeper into the relevance of using SOC  for these separations and purification  so for carbohydrates one of the methods  I've used for this is enhanced with  fluidity liquid chromatography otherwise  known as EF LC e f LC is used on SMC  instrumentation but the idea is that you  tend to use a higher modifier then you  would typically find SFC traditional SFC  methods use v the 50% modifier often  with methanol for polarity and the idea  is that as you have more  modifier you have higher pressure but  you exchanged that for higher illusion  strength meaning that for more polar  compounds the higher modifier enables  the illusion and therefore separation of  those analytes now in somewhat recent  times people have been more exploring  the use of methanol water combinations  to get even more solvent strength  however along with this you have even  more higher pressure to deal with so and  in addition to that not all proportions  of methanol water and co2 are miscible  and so you have to keep in mind that you  have to keep in mind the exact  proportions in which you mix for mobile  phase when you plan to use them however  if you're aware of the de mixing region  and you know where to use methanol water  and co2 you can exact decent separations  of polar molecules like sugars as shown  in the example below now the FLC has  been used many times in the analytical  scale but recently I published a paper  where we showed an example of using it  in the prep reduce Cal for purification  much like you would expect for hillock  style or SFC style of purification what  tends to happen is that the sample day  you went is very importantly solvent in  which you dissolve your sample prior to  injection is very important to the kind  of peak shape and the amount of bloating  that you can use on a particularly sized  column what you would expect is that as  you have higher organic content in your  dil youant you can usually load more and  you have better peak shape however in  the event that you are using EF LC you  are likely dealing with compounds that  are relatively hydrophilic or polar and  therefore you might find that it's  actually better to use a more aqueous or  even pure water dil you went and instead  leverage  low sample volume and high concentration  because you have higher solubility of  your analyte and aqueous a more aqueous  medium so it's important to keep in mind  that if you use EF LC for the purpose of  scale to realize that there is some  fine-tuning to do with sample WN  furthermore based off of this work with  privative purification in EF LC we  demonstrated that the method that  developed was fully amenable to mass  directed purification now this is very  important for us to see in that for HPLC  mass directed purification has been  around for some time  but in SFC it's still a it's still  instrumentation that it's not quite it's  not quite Universal and so it was very  impressive to see if they'll see a new  method be completely amenable with mass  directed purification what's good about  this is that you you can look you can  detect anomalies so if you can't see the  molecule or analyte of interest in UV  you can leverage the mass spec or for  triggering a purification furthermore  because you have a confirmed identity a  very selective identity of the analyte  of interest this allows some flexibility  of for separations that may not be a  robust meaning retention time is  shifting as you as you perform  subsequent runs and so with a master  activated system you can account for  this and furthermore because you have a  selective detector you can be sure that  what you are collecting is in fact the  analyte of interest so with EF LC we are  able to use mass directed purification  workflow now I will shift gears towards  another polar set of molecules  nucleotides and pharmaceutical analogs  now nucleotides are known to be very  troublesome in SOC and they continue to  be to this day  and you can kind of see that as you look  at the chemical structure of nucleotides  you start with a simple nucleobase  nucleobases can usually be separated  fairly easily on even just traditional  silica column using a methanol modifier  however when you add the carbohydrate  and turn that into a nucleoside you you  will find that you can still use SFC  style  conditions but a little water goes a  long way as far as improving peak shake  now when you add the phosphate group to  and you have nucleotides that you're  dealing with like adenosine  monophosphate your peak shape becomes  terrible and you have a lot of trouble  loading and so even with the strategy we  use for carbohydrates earlier using high  modifier and high amounts of water in  that methanolic modifier you still don't  have the peak shape necessary to have a  good analytical method or a good  preparators method so recently and  another patient that if you specifically  have a combination of methanol water and  ammonium hydroxide that's finely tuned  for the sake of SFC purification or  analytical scale methods you can  actually allude and separate nucleotides  i mean particularly a pharmaceutical  analogues with these nucleotide but we  took this even so far as to show a  preparatory r where we were able to  obtain the nucleotide of interest i  would like to also mention that there is  a known EF LC analytical method for  separating nucleobases new--this sorry  nucleosides and nucleotides however this  method does require the use of sodium  phosphate buffer so the next class of  molecules I'd like to talk about the  last one is peptides and proteins and I  would like to further delve into  particularly the effective SOC on global  conformation when looking at these  molecules so proteins and peptides again  covering their first  set of molecules which are all  constructed of amino acids but they can  have different levels of hydrophobicity  hydrophilicity they can have post  translational modification and  altogether be very complex molecules in  addition um you have another form of  structure to deal with higher order  structure that you typically don't see  with small molecules and this is  particularly important for the  pharmaceutical industry industry where  um you you we need drugs to remain  active after purification and part of  that activity oftentimes is related to  the confirmation of that protein  unfortunately SFC in today's literature  is known to cause protein denaturation  however is important to look at is the  denaturation caused by SFC reversible  perhaps um and there are many reasons  for instance that would cause SOC 2 to  denature protein namely could be  exposure to co2 two of the exposure to  high pressure and in general exposure to  an environment that is unlike what the  protein would normally see in its native  form so i'll go over a little history on  the separation of peptides and proteins  one of the fountains that one of the  determining works that showed that SFC  could be applied to peptides was done by  taylor terry taylor and co-workers and  in this case he showed that if you use  pure methanol as your modifier you  generally had very very very poor peak  shape unusable for analytical method  method design and if you added some  trifluoroacetic acid particularly anion  bearing agent you had much much better  peak shape that made SOC a viable  technique later to this I published a  paper using EF LC and mass spectrometry  in which I separated many peptides and  proteins going even as large as 80 kilo  daltons for transferring protein that is  also fairly polar I again  I used a methanol water a combination  with high modifier in order to exact  this kind of separation now when looking  at this EF lcms method i particularly  tried to probe whether the protein was  likely denatured or not and there was a  couple ways to look into this with a  protein as it goes to a mass spec it is  ionized and if it's folded or native  typically the charge envelope that you  would see is on the low side whereas if  you have a unfolded protein there is  more protons ionizable protons exposed  that can now be ionized by the mass spec  and therefore you'll have a higher  charge envelope in addition you can  track to see if the mass of the original  protein is preserved so that the protein  is in fact intact in my work we had  determined that for the select example  shown here  transferring cytochrome c the proteins  were intact but the charge states were  rather high indicating that there was  likely some unfolding involved now  fast-forward into a project at Merck and  we wanted to look at the use of SFC for  the sake of protein purification in this  case we devised a generic method for all  the peptides and proteins we tested  using a two Pico Lilla beam column to  centimeter prep and a special blend of  mobile phase in which the modifier was a  three to one methanol associate or nitro  ratio with of course using water and TFA  to enable good Pichet the idea here is  that we have a fast high cycle time bar  rather low cycle time method in which we  would expose the protein to actual  purification conditions so the idea here  is during a subsea pyrrha fication would  the unfolding that would most likely  happen be reversible for the proteins  and peptides that we wanted to test we  had these five analytes  starting with bradykinin which is a  peptide with no order and therefore what  we put into the SFC instrument would  likely be the same as what we would get  out versus myoglobin which is a much  more complex protein and may be  susceptible to donation during SFC  purification so one of the ways in which  you can you can probe for protein  conformation is circular dichroism now  this is the oftentimes viewed as the  gold standard for determining protein  conformation and they can look at  different secondary structures alpha  helixes Bettie beta sheets and random  random coil formation and in this case  we had all of our five peptides and  proteins checked both pre and post  purification as shown in the the read  further after PFC purification and the  black before purification the idea here  being that the profile should be roughly  the same if a protein or peptide is not  modified after purification in this case  all the peptides and proteins that had  order to begin with seem to lose that  order except for insulin which according  to our theoretical helical calculation  seemed to mostly preserve its its its  protein structure basically based on  this kind of analysis I will point out  Apple myoglobin is is though the  calculation shows a some difference that  this difference is slight um and would  oftentimes need to be verified by some  other method and furthermore even though  C D spectra at a particular solvent and  temperature can usually indicate that  protein conformation is similar it does  not necessarily guarantee that the  protein conformation is the same so for  this reason we have C D temper  temperature hysteresis analysis and in  in this form of probing what we do is we  have  protein sample that we heat up subject  to heat and we watch the protein unfold  and take measurements at every  temperature and then we have the protein  cool and this causes some refolding of  that protein um in perhaps a reversible  or irreversible way but the idea is if  you have two protein samples that are  the same then it should unfold and  refold in the same manner as you elevate  temperature and decrease temperature in  this example again insulin was the only  analyte which seemed to maintain its  secondary structure and just to bring  the focus over to Apple myoglobin you  can now more clearly see that the pre  and post purification protein has in  fact changed structure based off of this  analysis now the third way in which you  can look at protein conformation is  using a technique that we've developed  in our labs called size exclusion  chromatography hydrogen deuterium  exchange analysis now the idea with this  that you have a protein that has  ionizable protons and you can put this  protein in a deuterium oxide solvent  which allows you to do to rate these  ionizable protons and from a deuterated  protein you can then expose the protein  to size exclusion chromatography which  has a protic solvent used in it and the  protons in this protic solvent BAC  exchange with the deuterons on a  deuterated protein the idea here is that  a more unfolded protein will have more  due duration sites available for proton  BAC exchange and therefore you can  distinguish between a more and less  folded protein using the concept of  delta h DX which is basically the  difference  in molecular weight between a underrated  proton undoped rated protein and a  proton back exchanged protein from the  difference between these two you can  tell which one is more folded by the  idea that the more folded protein  because it has less ionizable less  ionizable protonation sites should have  more deuterons contained within it and  therefore the delta HDX should be higher  for that more folded protein and  conversely a protein that is more  unfolded will have the lower HDX so for  the same reason that you look at you  look at temperature changes in  temperature hysteresis analysis or  circular dichroism you would look at  temperature dependent HDX analysis just  to make sure that you have the same  profile between two protein samples in  particular from from this analysis we  can see that again myoglobin clearly has  different structure both for and after  purification before purification in  black again and the post purification in  read an interesting our situation that  we detect with myoglobin is that in fact  we see a higher Delta HDX which doesn't  mean that the protein is necessarily  more native but it does mean that the  protein is or compact or most likely  based off of this Delta HDX reading of  these again insulin is the only peptide  analyte that actually seem to maintain  its secondary structure or its global  structure throughout this whole analysis  and so the sec HDX data corroborates the  ECD and also data that we had determined  from the previous work so in conclusion  I hope I've convinced you  SFC can be used for a variety of  applications including the more polar  novel pharmaceutical applications that  we're seeing now furthermore a SOC can  be used with protein therapeutic  molecules but should be carefully probed  for the sake of seeing if conformation  changes during purification therefore  it's a profile 'ti of an SMC  purification method I'd also like to  mention that of the methods that you can  use for probing si si HDX certainly a  method as effective for determining  protein structure after SFC purification  however it would be best in order to use  all methods for presented here for the  sake of determining protein conformation  and for that as a visual example of this  I present to you the six men and the